    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study tests whether a decoding change—temperature-zero speculative decoding—changes safety-scored outputs, and reports a scoped null rather than the predicted bypass. It does not test the original fine-tuning, token-position, or parameter-localization claims.",
    "gate_note": "The study measures matched target-only versus speculative AdvBench refusal rates and reports results for several speculative-decoding configurations. Its fine-tuning citations are background, not results. The DPO-trained standalone draft result is included as a finding about an intentionally induced behavior, not as evidence that ordinary fine-tuning erodes safety.",
    "test_reason": "The study does not test any version of the claim. It asks whether temperature-zero speculative decoding, which is built to reproduce the target's own output, changes safety-scored outputs compared with target-only decoding. V4 says that forcing past the opening (prefill, suffixes, or decoding changes such as sampling settings) defeats refusal. Speculative decoding at T=0 neither forces nor alters the opening tokens; the target verifies every token. A null here does not show that opening-bypass decoding changes fail. So the matched refusal contrasts measure a different construct: invariance of an acceleration method. Qi et al. is cited only as background.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors report a bounded null: at T=0 in vLLM v0.19, speculative decoding shows no detectable safety divergence from target-only decoding under TAIS. They exclude sampling temperatures, other frameworks, other model families and tree variants, and treat the 70B run as a one-arm probe only.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "This is an inference-time speculative-decoding evaluation with matched target-only comparisons, not a fine-tuning, prefill, suffix, per-token KL, or weight-localization experiment.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The study varies acceptance method, model pair, draft construction (canonical, DPO-adversarial, GPTQ-4bit), seed, accumulator dtype, and speculation length. It reports that bf16 shifts 36%–53% of output bytes but keeps safety rates within equivalence; the other matched AdvBench refusal contrasts are reported as identical.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "At T=0 in vLLM v0.19, matched target-only vs speculative AdvBench refusal rates were equal or within 1pp (max |h|=0.024) across Llama/Qwen pairs. This held with rejection sampling or typical acceptance, adversarial, quantized or canonical drafts, two seeds, and bf16.",
        "proposed_claim": "Greedy speculative decoding preserves the target model's refusal behaviour, even with a harmful-preference draft.",
